xt pm turns toxic it's not  like they're going to be pretending to  be  bosom buddies um  how do you think  they are managing to  differentiate themselves rachel to you  first  yeah i think so you say it's very  obvious that this story debates the  leadership election has become very  bitter and increasingly so over the last  couple of weeks we saw it when penny  morgan was still in the race actually  the papers a couple of papers went for  her there was a lot of briefing against  her  and it seems like it's the shame with  this as well if you look across the  papers tomorrow there's briefings from  either side they're talking about tax  policy  immigration national security just  trying to be  different from one another or push the  other one down on the other one so you  know the stance on china for example is  used a lot in the papers tomorrow we're  pivoting away from tax and the economy  slightly and but i think there will be  this real push between um liz trust and  rishi sanek at the moment but the  problem with that is that actually this  election this leadership race so many  people in the party who have spoken to  senior politicians needed and wanted  this to be about unifying the party  because the last few months partygate  scandal and other issues with the party  really have not set the tories in a good  light in the rest of the uk and they  wanted this to be about the party coming  together but you know you just see this  attacks on each other one of these  people has to be the prime minister and  one of them will have to get in behind  that prime minister  and otherwise it's not going to be a  good look for them going into the next  general election so it's getting  increasingly better with six weeks left  to go you know how far is this going to  go that's interesting and peter in terms  of the tory members because that's  really who they need to be winning over  now uh  where are the most significant  divergences which give give them  something to hang their hat on in terms  of who they pick  is it the economy well it's quite  it's quite remarkable it's what you can  see from the front pages tonight and  then you've seen it really you know i  saw the star i think it said i'm gonna  have four more heat waves this summer  we're gonna definitely have six more  weeks of this kind of you know kind of  sniping attacks from soon i can trust  but what's quite the most striking i  think about this is that they seem so  detached from reality you know we're  talking about front pages headlines  about nhs chronic problems inflation  running ten percent and you have what  looked like really really niche  interests around the role of china and  british universities um tax cuts etc and  there clearly are yes this is how the  election now works where we our next  premise is going to be chosen by a very  very small selector of 170 000 tory  members and what we're seeing and what's  almost most interesting about the papers  today is is kind of where they're coming  from where you've got both the mail and  the express who clearly are very much in  team liz trust and are really putting  the boost into rishi sunak and i think  that's gonna be probably the tone of the  next few weeks is that the right wing  papers have clearly come out pretty  strongly for liz trust so while they  continue this they're going to go very  hard against uh sunak and meanwhile i  would expect the rest of the newspapers  are going to cover what are the really  big issues in this country at the moment  which are you know are pretty massive in  the climate to  the cost of living to the state of the  nhs and we're showing the front page of  the daily mail there rachel in china  um  i i guess the general public have a view  on whether that matters to them and i'd  imagine that it doesn't matter very much  at the moment compared to travel  disruption  nhs backlogs does it matter to tory  members  yes peter said there's so many issues  that are impacting people's lives just  now whether that's fossil living nhs  that people really want to hear about  but also as they said this is a very  small electorate that the that liz trust  and rishi surak are talking to and they  want to hear issues that they you know  we're going to be tough on they want to  hear that that the conservatives are  going to be tough on china they want to  hear these policies around immigration  and tax this is what they  believe that the membership want to hear  and i think you know it was an interest  it's an interesting move to pivot so  quickly we spoke about you know we  didn't really get into  when we first had the leadership race  the beginning of it kind of lacked  policy nobody was really talking about  what they wanted to do other than tax  cuts um but now we've kind of you know  wishy senick was very comfortable  talking about that those and i think liz  trust and her campaign have seen  the china comments that he's made today  about you know getting off on china and  the investment in uk universities from  the chinese state you know she's seen  this as something or people in her  campaign have come back at and said  you know  really like what are you talking about  and i think that they have seen this as  something that he might not be  as well kind of bred on as they hope  that liz trust's foreign secretary will  be  um but this is obviously something that  they believe that they are talking to a  very small electorate and that they will  like you know liz trust is very much  seen as the right-wing candidate in this  election  and rishi sunak is you know the the more  centrist he's very careful with tax cuts  for example but he's going to have to  kind of try and get himself back into  this race between poland figures where  liz tross is ahead of him and there will  be that you know push for wishes to  actually kind of lean more to the right  now  yeah and given that we're saying that  it's a niche  issue perhaps we won't go to the daily  telegraph which is also talking about  any even more niche aspect of this  talking as you were saying there about  liz truss's  involvement in  china's influence in the university  should we move on to something that i  think probably more people  could get excited about and not in a  good way and that's on the front of the  guardian  peter talking about this  colossal staffing crisis in the nhs  yes and there's been quite a bit of  trailing of this over the last few days  as well there's some stories in the  sunday times this morning about the  scale of the staffing crisis in the nhs  and i think like for a lot of people who  are engaging with the health service  these are the kind of issues that they  are going to be seeing and what we're  you know there's a big new briefiness  coming out from the um health committee  at the house commons which is chaired by  jeremy hunt but it's a cross-party  committee it's seen as very influential  it's seen broadly as not necessarily  being pro or anti-government so warning  like this coming from that committee is  seen as very significant and you know  it's not really a surprise there's been  many a decade plus of austerity a series  of cuts to the health service and what  we are seeing is a lot of people in  their everyday lives and interaction  with the health service are seeing the  the reality of the staff in crisis and  you would expect unfortunately this is  the summer we do have coldwood we have a  kind of you know we do have very hard  devils of coal but again some of that  seems to be going through into health  service but we are not at the winter yet  so you know in many ways a bit like the  cost of living pertains to uh heating  people's homes we're on the kind of easy  side of it compared to what we're seeing  likely coming down the track and if this  warning from coming from mp's anything  to go by the next few months will be  very very very hard for the nhs  um rachel whoever gets the health brief  in the new prime minister's  cabinet's going to have an almighty  great job on their hands aren't they  yeah this is absolutely one of the areas  that has to be top priority for a new  prime minister and they will need to put  health secretary in that can deal with  this you know it'll have to be someone  that is ready to take this on as peter  said you know this is summer the summer  is usually that quieter stretch the nhs  and we get into winter and all those  other viruses and illnesses that come on  top of um the usual pressures that the  nhs face but we don't only have that we  have ovid and we also have these huge  back blocks and delays from the padlock  as well when things were put off you  know there's still huge demand on the  nhs and it's only going to get worse  throughout the winter and i think  looking at some of the figures and there  was only figures for england in this  report but you know 12 000 hospital  doctors and 50 000 nurse and midwifery  vacancies in the nhs in england is  absolutely well those are incredible  figures um when you think about it and  this isn't you know the pressure on the  nhs is not just the pandemic either you  know there was already pressures on the  nhs this has been a long time coming and  it's obviously been made worse by the  pad limit but for any health secretary  this is something they really have to  get on top of as quickly as possible

